<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Class-Incremental Learning (CIL) requires models to learn new classes without forgetting old ones. A common method is to freeze a pre-trained model and train a new, lightweight adapter for each task. While this prevents forgetting, it treats the learned knowledge as a simple, unstructured collection and fails to use the relationships between tasks. To this end, we propose the Semantic-guided Adaptive Expert Forest (SAEF), a new method that organizes adapters into a structured hierarchy for better knowledge sharing. SAEF first groups tasks into conceptual clusters based on their semantic relationships. Then, within each cluster, it builds a balanced expert tree by creating new adapters from merging the adapters of similar tasks. At inference time, SAEF finds and activates a set of relevant experts from the forest for any given input. The final prediction is made by combining the outputs of these activated experts, weighted by how confident each expert is. Experiments on several benchmark datasets show that SAEF achieves SOTA performance.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S0.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.20911v1/over.png" id="S0.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/477;" width="685" height="477" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S0.F1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S0.F1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Conceptual comparison of adapter-based CIL methods.
<span id="S0.F1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(a)</span> The conventional methods treat adapters as an unstructured pool, leading to inefficient full-ensemble inference.
<span id="S0.F1.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(b)</span> Our proposed SAEF automatically organizes adapters into a semantic expert forest, enabling an efficient, adaptive search for the most relevant experts.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Class-Incremental Learning (CIL) enables models to continually acquire knowledge of new classes while preserving performance on previously learned ones <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. While mitigating catastrophic forgetting is a primary objective, the growing application of CIL in resource-sensitive contexts, such as on-device and robotic systems, has also underscored the importance of computational efficiency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>.
CIL methodologies can be broadly categorized into two paradigms: training from scratch and leveraging Pre-trained Models (PTMs). The former category, which includes regularization, architectural, and rehearsal-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, often encounters practical limitations concerning scalability, data privacy, and overall performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>. Consequently, a significant portion of recent research has shifted towards PTM-based CIL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>. This method utilizes the powerful representations learned during large-scale pre-training as a robust foundation for incremental tasks, frequently leading to state-of-the-art (SOTA) results. A prominent strategy within this paradigm involves freezing the PTM backbone and applying Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) techniques, such as training lightweight adapters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> or prompts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>. This method offers a compelling balance of high performance and low computational overhead, making it a highly influential and widely adopted method in the field.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A common strategy within this PTM-based paradigm is to train a separate, lightweight adapter for each incremental task <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>. During inference, systems may either retrieve the most suitable task adapter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> or ensemble predictions from the entire collection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>. While separating task-specific parameters helps prevent catastrophic forgetting, this method treats the learned knowledge as a simple, unstructured collection, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ From Isolation to Integration: Building an Adaptive Expert Forest for Pre-Trained Model-based Class-Incremental Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(a). This design fails to use the important semantic relationships between tasks (for example, between a ’dog’ task and a ’cat’ task) for better knowledge transfer. Some methods attempt to address this by adding a global module to learn shared knowledge from all tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. However, we argue that sharing knowledge globally across all tasks is a suboptimal method. True knowledge transfer should be more selective and structured. It should be context-specific and hierarchical, sharing information only between related tasks, as illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ From Isolation to Integration: Building an Adaptive Expert Forest for Pre-Trained Model-based Class-Incremental Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(b).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To this end, we propose the Semantic-guided Adaptive Expert Forest (SAEF). SAEF is a method that automatically organizes the set of task-specific adapters into a structured expert forest for better knowledge sharing. This process consists of three main stages. First, SAEF groups tasks into conceptual clusters based on their semantic relationships. Second, within each cluster, it constructs a balanced expert tree by recursively creating new adapters from merging the adapters of the most similar tasks. This process builds a hierarchy of experts at different levels of abstraction. Finally, the third stage involves adaptive inference, where SAEF employs a parallel search strategy across this hierarchy to dynamically activate a subset of relevant experts for any given sample. The final prediction is produced by an entropy-guided fusion of these experts’ outputs. In summary, our main contributions are as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose SAEF, a novel method that automatically constructs a semantic expert hierarchy from previously isolated task adapters to enable structured knowledge reuse.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We design a dynamic inference strategy that adaptively navigates this hierarchy, using a multi-path search and an entropy-guided fusion mechanism to ensure robust predictions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct extensive experiments on major CIL benchmarks, where SAEF consistently outperforms SOTA methods with accuracy gains of up to 1.34%.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">CIL methods can be broadly divided into two main categories: training models from scratch and using Pre-trained Models (PTMs).</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Training Models from Scratch</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The traditional method for CIL is to train a model from scratch. These methods are typically grouped into three categories. <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Regularization-based</span> methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> add a loss term to penalize changes to important parameters from past tasks. <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Architecture-based</span> methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite> add new parameters for each new task to keep knowledge separate, for example, by growing the network or using different parts of it for different tasks. <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rehearsal-based</span> methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> store a small number of old samples and replay them during training on new tasks. These methods have limitations in scalability and performance, which has led to a shift towards PTM-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Pre-Trained Model-based CIL</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To avoid the high cost of training from scratch, modern CIL often relies on a frozen pre-trained model (PTM) with Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT). This involves training only a small set of new parameters for each task. This makes CIL practical for many resource-constrained applications. Common PEFT methods include prompt tuning, which learns to select or combine prompts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, and adapter-based tuning, which adds a new adapter for each task <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>. Other methods also follow this paradigm, such as fine-tuning only the final layer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite> or using random projections <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, the focus has shifted towards enabling knowledge sharing between these task-specific modules. For instance, some methods learn to route inputs using a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, while others create a shared global expert <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>. A key limitation of these methods, however, is that they treat the set of experts or adapters as an unstructured collection. This overlooks the underlying semantic hierarchy of tasks, where some tasks are inherently more related than others. Our work addresses this exact limitation by proposing a method that organizes adapters based on their semantic relationships, enabling more structured and effective knowledge sharing.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methodology</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Preliminaries</h3>

<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Class-Incremental Learning (CIL).</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Class-Incremental Learning, a model learns from a sequence of tasks, where each new task introduces a new set of classes. We consider a sequence of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> tasks, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathcal{D}_{1},\mathcal{D}_{2},\ldots,\mathcal{D}_{T}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathcal{D}_{1},\mathcal{D}_{2},\ldots,\mathcal{D}_{T}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each task <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the model is given a training set <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{t}=\{(x_{i},y_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{t}=\{(x_{i},y_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is an image and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is its class label from the set <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. The sets of classes are disjoint for different tasks, so <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{t}\cap\mathcal{C}_{t^{\prime}}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{t}\cap\mathcal{C}_{t^{\prime}}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\neq t^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\neq t^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">During the training phase for task <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the model only has access to the data in <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Our work follows the challenging exemplar-free setting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>, meaning no samples from past tasks are stored for replay. The model consists of a feature extractor <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> and a classifier <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>. The set of all trainable parameters for task <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted as <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. After learning from task <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the model’s performance is evaluated on all classes seen so far, which are in the set <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}_{t}=\bigcup_{i=1}^{t}\mathcal{C}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}_{t}=\bigcup_{i=1}^{t}\mathcal{C}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The main goal is to find the optimal parameters <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> that minimize the loss over the test sets of all tasks:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t}^{*}=\underset{\theta_{t}}{\operatorname{argmin}}\sum_{i=1}^{t}\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim\mathcal{D}^{\text{test}}_{i}}\left[\mathcal{L}_{\text{CE}}(W^{\top}\phi(x;\theta_{t}),y)\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>argmin</mi><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">θ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">t</mi></msub></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msubsup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>CE</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t}^{*}=\underset{\theta_{t}}{\operatorname{argmin}}\sum_{i=1}^{t}\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim\mathcal{D}^{\text{test}}_{i}}\left[\mathcal{L}_{\text{CE}}(W^{\top}\phi(x;\theta_{t}),y)\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{\top}\phi(x;\theta_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{\top}\phi(x;\theta_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> are the output logits from the model, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\text{test}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\text{test}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the test set for the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th task, and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{CE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>CE</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{CE}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the standard cross-entropy loss. A successful CIL model needs to be plastic enough to learn new classes from <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> while remaining stable to avoid forgetting the old classes in <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">PTM-based CIL with Task-Specific Adapters.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our method is based on the modern PTM-based CIL framework. In this framework, a large pre-trained model, like a ViT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, is used as a fixed backbone. For each new task, only a small, lightweight adapter module is trained. This partitions the trainable parameters <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> into a set of task-specific adapters, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a classifier that is updated for new classes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Following standard practice <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>, adapters are small bottleneck modules inserted into each Transformer block of the PTM. An adapter is usually added in parallel to the MLP layer. For an input feature <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> to the MLP, the forward pass with an adapter is computed as:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{x}_{o}=\text{MLP}(\mathbf{x}_{i})+\text{ReLU}(\mathbf{x}_{i}W_{\text{down}})W_{\text{up}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>MLP</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mtext>ReLU</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mtext>down</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mtext>up</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{x}_{o}=\text{MLP}(\mathbf{x}_{i})+\text{ReLU}(\mathbf{x}_{i}W_{\text{down}})W_{\text{up}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{o}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{o}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is the output. The adapter consists of a down-projection matrix <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\text{down}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mtext>down</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\text{down}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> and an up-projection matrix <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\text{up}}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mtext>up</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\text{up}}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the bottleneck dimension <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is much smaller than <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. For each new task <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, a new adapter <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{t}=\{W_{\text{down}}^{(l,t)},W_{\text{up}}^{(l,t)}\}_{l=1}^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mtext>down</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mtext>up</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{t}=\{W_{\text{down}}^{(l,t)},W_{\text{up}}^{(l,t)}\}_{l=1}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is initialized and trained.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To reduce interference between different task adapters, the training process for <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> on task <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> combines two loss functions. The first is the standard cross-entropy classification loss, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{cls}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>cls</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{cls}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is computed over all classes seen so far, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{cls}}=-\frac{1}{N_{t}}\sum_{(\mathbf{x},y)\in\mathcal{D}_{t}}\log\frac{\exp(\mathbf{w}_{y}^{\top}\phi(\mathbf{x};\mathcal{A}_{t}))}{\sum_{c\in\mathcal{Y}_{t}}\exp(\mathbf{w}_{c}^{\top}\phi(\mathbf{x};\mathcal{A}_{t}))}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>cls</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mi>log</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{cls}}=-\frac{1}{N_{t}}\sum_{(\mathbf{x},y)\in\mathcal{D}_{t}}\log\frac{\exp(\mathbf{w}_{y}^{\top}\phi(\mathbf{x};\mathcal{A}_{t}))}{\sum_{c\in\mathcal{Y}_{t}}\exp(\mathbf{w}_{c}^{\top}\phi(\mathbf{x};\mathcal{A}_{t}))}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(\mathbf{x};\mathcal{A}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(\mathbf{x};\mathcal{A}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is the feature vector produced by the model with the new adapter <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> is the classifier weight for class <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>. The second loss is an orthogonality regularization term, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{orth}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>orth</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{orth}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which encourages the up-projection matrix of the new adapter to be orthogonal to those of all previous adapters:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{orth}}=\sum_{l=1}^{L}\sum_{i=1}^{t-1}\left\|{W_{\text{up}}^{(l,t)}}\cdot{(W_{\text{up}}^{(l,i)})}^{\top}\right\|_{F}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>orth</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mtext>up</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mtext>up</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{orth}}=\sum_{l=1}^{L}\sum_{i=1}^{t-1}\left\|{W_{\text{up}}^{(l,t)}}\cdot{(W_{\text{up}}^{(l,i)})}^{\top}\right\|_{F},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\text{up}}^{(l,t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mtext>up</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\text{up}}^{(l,t)}</annotation></semantics></math> is the up-projection matrix of the adapter in the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>-th layer for the current task <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Frobenius norm. The overall loss for training the adapter <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a weighted sum of these two components:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{cls}}+\lambda\mathcal{L}_{\text{orth}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>cls</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>orth</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{cls}}+\lambda\mathcal{L}_{\text{orth}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is a hyperparameter that balances the two objectives.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.20911v1/method.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/370;" width="685" height="370" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Overview of the SAEF.
Our method organizes individually trained task adapters into a structured knowledge hierarchy.
<span id="S3.F2.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1) Stage 1: Conceptual Clustering.</span> Based on semantic prototypes, tasks are grouped into high-level conceptual clusters (e.g., ’Animal’, ’Vehicle’).
<span id="S3.F2.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2) Stage 2: Hierarchical Construction.</span> Within each cluster, a balanced expert tree is built via a bottom-up process of recursively fusing the most visually similar nodes. The roots of these trees are then merged to form a single Global Expert.
<span id="S3.F2.7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(3) Stage 3: Adaptive Inference.</span> For a given input, SAEF performs a parallel, multi-path search to dynamically activate a set of relevant experts. The final prediction is derived from an entropy-guided fusion of their outputs.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Our Method: Semantic-guided Adaptive Expert Forest</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our method begins by following the standard PTM-based CIL paradigm, where a separate, task-specific adapter is trained for each incremental task using the objective in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ PTM-based CIL with Task-Specific Adapters. ‣ 3.1 Preliminaries ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ From Isolation to Integration: Building an Adaptive Expert Forest for Pre-Trained Model-based Class-Incremental Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). However, instead of treating the resulting set of adapters as a simple, flat collection during inference, we propose the Semantic-guided Adaptive Expert Forest (SAEF). SAEF is a method that organizes these isolated adapters into a structured, hierarchical knowledge base as new tasks are learned. This process consists of three main stages, as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ PTM-based CIL with Task-Specific Adapters. ‣ 3.1 Preliminaries ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ From Isolation to Integration: Building an Adaptive Expert Forest for Pre-Trained Model-based Class-Incremental Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>: <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Conceptual Clustering</span>, <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hierarchical Construction</span>, and <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Adaptive Inference</span>. We describe each stage in detail below.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Conceptual Clustering.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recognizing that incremental tasks often fall into distinct conceptual domains (e.g., distinguishing ’animals’ from ’vehicles’), the first stage of SAEF partitions the set of all <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> learned tasks into semantically coherent groups. To achieve this, we first define two complementary prototypes for each task <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>: a semantic prototype <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{t}^{\text{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>s</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{t}^{\text{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> and a visual prototype <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{t}^{\text{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>f</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{t}^{\text{f}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Semantic prototypes are derived using a pre-trained and frozen text encoder, denoted as <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> from CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>. For each class name <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> belonging to a task, we construct a corresponding text prompt <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> using the template ”a photo of a [CLASS]”. The semantic prototype for task <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is then the mean of these text embeddings:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{t}^{\text{s}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{C}_{t}|}\sum_{c\in\mathcal{C}_{t}}g_{t}(t_{c})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>s</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{t}^{\text{s}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{C}_{t}|}\sum_{c\in\mathcal{C}_{t}}g_{t}(t_{c}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Visual prototypes are extracted using the PTM’s feature extractor <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>, in conjunction with the adapter from the first incremental task <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. We use this combination <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(\cdot;\mathcal{A}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(\cdot;\mathcal{A}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, because <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> helps to adapt the general pre-trained features to the specific downstream domain, effectively bridging the domain gap. The visual prototype for task <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is the mean feature vector of its training samples:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{t}^{\text{f}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}_{t}|}\sum_{\mathbf{x}_{i}\in\mathcal{D}_{t}}\phi(\mathbf{x}_{i};\mathcal{A}_{1})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>f</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{t}^{\text{f}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}_{t}|}\sum_{\mathbf{x}_{i}\in\mathcal{D}_{t}}\phi(\mathbf{x}_{i};\mathcal{A}_{1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">With the semantic prototypes <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{p}_{t}^{\text{s}}\}_{t=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>s</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{p}_{t}^{\text{s}}\}_{t=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> collected, we apply the K-Means algorithm to partition the set of task indices <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\ldots,T\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\ldots,T\}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> conceptual clusters, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{G_{1},\ldots,G_{K}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{G_{1},\ldots,G_{K}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The optimal number of clusters is determined automatically by selecting the value that maximizes the average silhouette score <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> over a predefined search range. This clustering process establishes the ’forest’ structure of SAEF. Each cluster <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> provides the initial set of task adapters that will be structured into a distinct ’tree’ in the <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hierarchical Construction</span> stage, and the visual prototypes <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{t}^{\text{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>f</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{t}^{\text{f}}</annotation></semantics></math> are used to guide this process.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Hierarchical Construction.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The initial clustering provides a coarse-grained organization of tasks. To enable more effective, fine-grained knowledge transfer, this second stage constructs a balanced binary tree of experts for each conceptual cluster <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then merges their roots into a single global expert. This transforms the initially unstructured collection of adapters into a unified, multi-level hierarchy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each cluster <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, we start with a set of leaf experts, where each expert corresponds to a single task <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in G_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in G_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and holds its adapter parameters <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> and visual prototype <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{t}^{\text{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>f</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{t}^{\text{f}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The tree is built using a bottom-up, iterative process. At each step, we find the two most similar experts in the current set by computing the cosine similarity between their visual prototypes:</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{sim}(i,j)=\frac{{\mathbf{p}_{i}^{\text{f}}}^{\top}\mathbf{p}_{j}^{\text{f}}}{\|\mathbf{p}_{i}^{\text{f}}\|_{2}\|\mathbf{p}_{j}^{\text{f}}\|_{2}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>sim</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>f</mtext><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>j</mi><mtext>f</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>f</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>j</mi><mtext>f</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{sim}(i,j)=\frac{{\mathbf{p}_{i}^{\text{f}}}^{\top}\mathbf{p}_{j}^{\text{f}}}{\|\mathbf{p}_{i}^{\text{f}}\|_{2}\|\mathbf{p}_{j}^{\text{f}}\|_{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the L2-norm. Once the most similar pair is identified, a new parent expert is created. The parent’s parameter vector, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{p}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{p}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, is formed by merging the parameters of its two children, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, using a vectorized operation designed to preserve the dominant signal from both:</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{p}\leftarrow\text{sign}(\theta_{i}+\theta_{j})\odot\max(|\theta_{i}|,|\theta_{j}|)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>sign</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{p}\leftarrow\text{sign}(\theta_{i}+\theta_{j})\odot\max(|\theta_{i}|,|\theta_{j}|),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{sign}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>sign</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{sign}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the element-wise sign function, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="|\cdot|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\cdot|</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> are element-wise operations for absolute value and maximum, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\odot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\odot</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Hadamard product. The visual prototype for the new parent is computed as a weighted average of its children’s prototypes, with weights proportional to the number of original leaf tasks each child represents. The two child experts are then replaced by their new parent in the set. This process continues until only a single root expert, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, remains for the cluster. The detailed procedure for prototype merging and managing the set of experts is provided in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Hierarchical Construction Details ‣ Appendix A Detailed Implementation of SAEF Operations ‣ From Isolation to Integration: Building an Adaptive Expert Forest for Pre-Trained Model-based Class-Incremental Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, to create a single entry point for inference, we merge the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> tree roots into one global root expert <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>. The parameters of this global root, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{G}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{G}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, are formed using a vectorized operation that captures both the consensus and the maximum signal strength from all trees:</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{G}\leftarrow\text{sign}\left(\sum_{k=1}^{K}\theta_{R_{k}}\right)\odot\max_{k\in\{1,\ldots,K\}}|\theta_{R_{k}}|," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>sign</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><msub><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{G}\leftarrow\text{sign}\left(\sum_{k=1}^{K}\theta_{R_{k}}\right)\odot\max_{k\in\{1,\ldots,K\}}|\theta_{R_{k}}|,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{sign}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>sign</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{sign}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the element-wise sign function, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> finds the element-wise maximum across the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> root vectors, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\odot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\odot</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Hadamard product. This completes the <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hierarchical Construction</span> of the full SAEF hierarchy.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Adaptive Inference.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">SAEF uses a dynamic inference strategy to classify any given input <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. The strategy finds an optimal path in each of the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> trees and then fuses the predictions from all experts along these paths.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For any expert in the hierarchy, represented by its adapter <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, we first compute its class-wise logits for a given input <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, the logit for each class <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in\mathcal{Y}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in\mathcal{Y}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed as <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{n}(c)=\mathbf{w}_{c}^{\top}\phi(x;\mathcal{A}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{n}(c)=\mathbf{w}_{c}^{\top}\phi(x;\mathcal{A}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is the classifier weight for class <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>. These logits are then transformed into a predictive distribution <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> via the softmax function:</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{n}(c)=\frac{\exp(s_{n}(c))}{\sum_{j\in\mathcal{Y}_{T}}\exp(s_{n}(j))}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{n}(c)=\frac{\exp(s_{n}(c))}{\sum_{j\in\mathcal{Y}_{T}}\exp(s_{n}(j))}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The expert’s predictive confidence is inversely measured by the entropy of this distribution:</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H(z_{n})=-\sum_{c\in\mathcal{Y}_{T}}z_{n}(c)\log z_{n}(c)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(z_{n})=-\sum_{c\in\mathcal{Y}_{T}}z_{n}(c)\log z_{n}(c).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The inference process begins with a parallel search, performed independently for each of the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> trees. Starting from each tree’s root, the search path follows the child expert that shows lower predictive entropy for the given input. This recursive selection defines an optimal inference path <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> from root to leaf for each tree. Further details on this entropy-guided selection process are provided in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Adaptive Inference Details ‣ Appendix A Detailed Implementation of SAEF Operations ‣ From Isolation to Integration: Building an Adaptive Expert Forest for Pre-Trained Model-based Class-Incremental Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The final prediction is derived from an activated set of experts <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> which includes the global root <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> and all experts from the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> identified paths:</p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}=\{R_{G}\}\cup\bigcup_{k=1}^{K}\mathcal{P}_{k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">∪</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}=\{R_{G}\}\cup\bigcup_{k=1}^{K}\mathcal{P}_{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">The fusion weight <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> for each activated expert is based on its confidence (negative entropy), normalized across the set with a temperature <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{n}=\frac{\exp(-H(z_{n})/\tau)}{\sum_{j\in\mathcal{E}}\exp(-H(z_{j})/\tau)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></msub><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{n}=\frac{\exp(-H(z_{n})/\tau)}{\sum_{j\in\mathcal{E}}\exp(-H(z_{j})/\tau)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p4.3" class="ltx_p">The final predictive distribution <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is the weighted average of the predictions from all activated experts:</p>
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{f}=\sum_{n\in\mathcal{E}}w_{n}\mathbf{z}_{n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{f}=\sum_{n\in\mathcal{E}}w_{n}\mathbf{z}_{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p4.4" class="ltx_p">This strategy allows SAEF to use knowledge from different levels of the hierarchy, weighting each expert’s opinion by its confidence to make a robust final prediction.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.T1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Average (<math id="S3.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>) and final task (<math id="S3.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>) Top-1 accuracy comparison on four challenging class-incremental learning benchmarks.
All methods use ViT-B/16-IN21K as the backbone and are evaluated in the exemplar-free setting.
The best performance in each column is highlighted in <span id="S3.T1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span>.
</span></figcaption>
<table id="S3.T1.6" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S3.T1.6.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.6.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"><span id="S3.T1.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.6.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" colspan="2"><span id="S3.T1.6.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CIFAR-100</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.6.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" colspan="2"><span id="S3.T1.6.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ImageNet-R</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.6.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" colspan="2"><span id="S3.T1.6.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ImageNet-A</span></th>
