             <math id="algx2.l33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{R}\leftarrow W_{T}^{\top}\phi(x;\mathcal{A}_{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{R}\leftarrow W_{T}^{\top}\phi(x;\mathcal{A}_{R})</annotation></semantics></math>;  <math id="algx2.l33.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{R}\leftarrow\text{Entropy}(\text{Softmax}(s_{R}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext>Entropy</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>Softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{R}\leftarrow\text{Entropy}(\text{Softmax}(s_{R}))</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algx2.l34" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algx2.l34.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">34:</span></span>
            
            
             <span id="algx2.l34.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="algx2.l34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{L}&lt;H_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{L}&lt;H_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="algx2.l34.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>

</div>
<div id="algx2.l35" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algx2.l35.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">35:</span></span>
            
            
               <span id="algx2.l35.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="algx2.l35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{node\}\cup\text{FindPath}(n_{L},x,\phi,W_{T},\tau_{e})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mtext>FindPath</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{node\}\cup\text{FindPath}(n_{L},x,\phi,W_{T},\tau_{e})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algx2.l36" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algx2.l36.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">36:</span></span>
            
            
             <span id="algx2.l36.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">else</span>
</div>
<div id="algx2.l37" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algx2.l37.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">37:</span></span>
            
            
               <span id="algx2.l37.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="algx2.l37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{node\}\cup\text{FindPath}(n_{R},x,\phi,W_{T},\tau_{e})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mtext>FindPath</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{node\}\cup\text{FindPath}(n_{R},x,\phi,W_{T},\tau_{e})</annotation></semantics></math>
     
</div>
</div>
</figure>
<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Hierarchical Construction Details</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The tree construction process operates on a set of nodes, where each expert in our hierarchy is represented as a node. Each node is a data structure containing its expert adapter, its visual prototype, and pointers to its children (if any).</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Node Representation.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A leaf node, corresponding to an initial task <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, is initialized as <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="node_{t}=(\mathcal{A}_{t},\mathbf{p}_{t}^{\text{f}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>f</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">node_{t}=(\mathcal{A}_{t},\mathbf{p}_{t}^{\text{f}})</annotation></semantics></math>. A parent node <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="node_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">node_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, created by merging two children (<math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="node_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">node_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="node_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">node_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>), has the structure <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="node_{p}=(\mathcal{A}_{p},\mathbf{p}_{p}^{\text{f}},node_{i},node_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>p</mi><mtext>f</mtext></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">node_{p}=(\mathcal{A}_{p},\mathbf{p}_{p}^{\text{f}},node_{i},node_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Node Selection.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As mentioned in the main text, tree construction is an iterative process that operates on a working set of nodes, <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, for each cluster <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. At each step, we identify the two most visually similar nodes, <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="node_{i}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">node_{i}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="node_{j}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">node_{j}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, by selecting the pair from <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> that maximizes the cosine similarity between their visual prototypes. The formal selection rule is:</p>
<table id="A1.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(node_{i}^{*},node_{j}^{*})=\mathop{\arg\max}_{node_{i},node_{j}\in\mathcal{N}_{k},i\neq j}\frac{{\mathbf{p}_{i}^{\text{f}}}^{\top}\mathbf{p}_{j}^{\text{f}}}{\|\mathbf{p}_{i}^{\text{f}}\|_{2}\|\mathbf{p}_{j}^{\text{f}}\|_{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>f</mtext><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>j</mi><mtext>f</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>f</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>j</mi><mtext>f</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(node_{i}^{*},node_{j}^{*})=\mathop{\arg\max}_{node_{i},node_{j}\in\mathcal{N}_{k},i\neq j}\frac{{\mathbf{p}_{i}^{\text{f}}}^{\top}\mathbf{p}_{j}^{\text{f}}}{\|\mathbf{p}_{i}^{\text{f}}\|_{2}\|\mathbf{p}_{j}^{\text{f}}\|_{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Prototype Merging.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When a new parent node is created from children <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="node_{i}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">node_{i}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="node_{j}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">node_{j}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, its visual prototype <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{p}^{\text{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>p</mi><mtext>f</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{p}^{\text{f}}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed as a weighted average of its children’s prototypes. The weights are proportional to the number of original leaf-level tasks that each child node represents. Let <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(node)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(node)</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of all leaf tasks that are descendants of a given <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="node" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">node</annotation></semantics></math>. The parent prototype is then formally defined as:</p>
<table id="A1.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{p}^{\text{f}}=\frac{|C(node_{i}^{*})|\cdot\mathbf{p}_{i^{*}}^{\text{f}}+|C(node_{j}^{*})|\cdot\mathbf{p}_{j^{*}}^{\text{f}}}{|C(node_{i}^{*})|+|C(node_{j}^{*})|}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>p</mi><mtext>f</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐩</mi><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mtext>f</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐩</mi><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mtext>f</mtext></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{p}^{\text{f}}=\frac{|C(node_{i}^{*})|\cdot\mathbf{p}_{i^{*}}^{\text{f}}+|C(node_{j}^{*})|\cdot\mathbf{p}_{j^{*}}^{\text{f}}}{|C(node_{i}^{*})|+|C(node_{j}^{*})|}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This weighting scheme ensures that the parent’s prototype is more influenced by the child that represents a larger body of knowledge. After the merge, <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="node_{i}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">node_{i}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="node_{j}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">node_{j}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> are removed from the working set <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the new parent node is added.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Adaptive Inference Details</h3>

<section id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Entropy-Guided Path Selection.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">During inference, SAEF performs a search to find the optimal path in each of the <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> trees. The search begins at the root of a tree and recursively descends to one of its children. At any non-leaf node (indexed by <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>) with children (indexed by <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> for left and right), the search proceeds to the child node that exhibits lower predictive entropy for the given input sample <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. The formal rule for selecting the next node in the path is:</p>
<table id="A1.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="node_{\text{next}}=\mathop{\arg\min}_{n\in\{node_{L},node_{R}\}}H(\mathbf{z}_{n})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mtext>next</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">node_{\text{next}}=\mathop{\arg\min}_{n\in\{node_{L},node_{R}\}}H(\mathbf{z}_{n}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\mathbf{z}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\mathbf{z}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is the Shannon entropy of the prediction from the expert at node <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, as defined in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E12" title="Equation 12 ‣ Adaptive Inference. ‣ 3.2 Our Method: Semantic-guided Adaptive Expert Forest ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ From Isolation to Integration: Building an Adaptive Expert Forest for Pre-Trained Model-based Class-Incremental Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>). This process is repeated until a leaf node is reached, defining a complete path from root to leaf.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Prototype-based Classifier and Alignment</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our method relies on a non-parametric classifier constructed from class prototypes. Here, we detail how these prototypes are created and aligned across different task-specific feature spaces.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Classifier Construction for New Classes.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any new class <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> introduced in the current task <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> with training samples <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, its prototype <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed as the mean feature vector of its samples, extracted using the current task’s adapter <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A1.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{c}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}_{c}|}\sum_{x\in\mathcal{D}_{c}}\phi(x;\mathcal{A}_{t})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{c}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}_{c}|}\sum_{x\in\mathcal{D}_{c}}\phi(x;\mathcal{A}_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The corresponding classifier weight <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is simply its L2-normalized prototype: <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{c}=\mathbf{p}_{c}/\|\mathbf{p}_{c}\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{c}=\mathbf{p}_{c}/\|\mathbf{p}_{c}\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Classifier Alignment for Old Classes.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This non-parametric method, however, introduces the subspace misalignment problem: prototypes computed for different tasks reside in distinct, incompatible feature subspaces generated by their respective adapters. To overcome this challenge without storing past exemplars, we adopt a widely-used alignment strategy inspired by methods like MOS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>. The process involves two steps:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="A1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="A1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Store Distributional Statistics:</span> For each past class <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, we model and store its feature distribution as a multivariate Gaussian, <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(\mu_{c^{\prime}},\Sigma_{c^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(\mu_{c^{\prime}},\Sigma_{c^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math>. These statistics are computed from the features extracted during that class’s initial training.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Re-estimate Prototypes in New Subspace:</span> When learning a new task <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, we generate pseudo-features by sampling from these stored distributions. These pseudo-features are then projected through the new task’s adapter, <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, to re-estimate the prototypes for all past classes within the new, unified subspace.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This procedure yields a fully aligned classifier head, <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{t}=[\mathbf{w}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{w}_{|\mathcal{Y}_{t}|}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{t}=[\mathbf{w}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{w}_{|\mathcal{Y}_{t}|}]</annotation></semantics></math>, where all class weights are comparable within the same feature space, enabling a fair comparison of logits during prediction.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>SAEF Algorithm Pseudocode</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To provide a clear and procedural overview of our method, we present the SAEF method as two separate algorithms. Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Appendix A Detailed Implementation of SAEF Operations ‣ From Isolation to Integration: Building an Adaptive Expert Forest for Pre-Trained Model-based Class-Incremental Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> details the offline <span id="A2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">hierarchy construction</span> phase, which is performed once after all tasks have been learned. Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ Appendix A Detailed Implementation of SAEF Operations ‣ From Isolation to Integration: Building an Adaptive Expert Forest for Pre-Trained Model-based Class-Incremental Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> details the online <span id="A2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">adaptive inference</span> phase, which is executed for each test sample.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F1.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20911v1/cifar_100.svg" id="A2.F1.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/451;" width="685" height="451"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F1.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="A2.F1.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">CIFAR-100</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F1.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20911v1/imagenet_r.svg" id="A2.F1.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/451;" width="685" height="451"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F1.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="A2.F1.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">ImageNet-R</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F1.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20911v1/imagenet_a.svg" id="A2.F1.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/451;" width="685" height="451"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F1.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="A2.F1.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">ImageNet-A</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F1.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20911v1/objectnet.svg" id="A2.F1.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/451;" width="685" height="451"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F1.sf4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(d)</span> </span><span id="A2.F1.sf4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">ObjectNet</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure A1</span>: </span><span id="A2.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Incremental accuracy trends of SAEF compared to SOTA CIL methods on four standard benchmarks: CIFAR-100, ImageNet-R, ImageNet-A, and ObjectNet. Across all scenarios, SAEF demonstrates consistently superior performance. This highlights its robust ability to mitigate catastrophic forgetting and maintain a clear advantage as the number of learned classes increases.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20911v1/random.svg" id="A2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/381;" width="548" height="381"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure A2</span>: </span><span id="A2.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Performance stability across multiple random seeds on ImageNet-R (10-task setting).
The plot displays the average accuracy (<math id="A2.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>) as the number of learned tasks increases.
All results shown are the mean performance calculated over five different random seeds, which vary the order of incoming classes.
The consistent and superior performance of SAEF highlights its robustness against variations in the incremental learning sequence.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Algorithmic Details</h3>

<section id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Offline Hierarchy Construction.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Appendix A Detailed Implementation of SAEF Operations ‣ From Isolation to Integration: Building an Adaptive Expert Forest for Pre-Trained Model-based Class-Incremental Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> details the offline process for organizing learned adapters into the SAEF hierarchy. The <span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BuildHierarchy</span> procedure encapsulates this entire workflow. It begins by computing the semantic and visual prototypes for all tasks (Lines 4-8), following Eq. (<a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ Conceptual Clustering. ‣ 3.2 Our Method: Semantic-guided Adaptive Expert Forest ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ From Isolation to Integration: Building an Adaptive Expert Forest for Pre-Trained Model-based Class-Incremental Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) and Eq. (<a href="#S3.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ Conceptual Clustering. ‣ 3.2 Our Method: Semantic-guided Adaptive Expert Forest ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ From Isolation to Integration: Building an Adaptive Expert Forest for Pre-Trained Model-based Class-Incremental Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>). Based on the semantic prototypes, it automatically determines the optimal number of clusters <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and partitions the tasks (Lines 9-10). Subsequently, it iterates through each cluster to build a dedicated expert tree using the <span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BuildTree</span> helper function (Lines 13-15). The <span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BuildTree</span> procedure (Lines 21-28) implements the iterative, bottom-up merging process. At each step, it identifies and merges the most visually similar pair of nodes (Lines 23-26), guided by Eq. (<a href="#S3.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ Hierarchical Construction. ‣ 3.2 Our Method: Semantic-guided Adaptive Expert Forest ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ From Isolation to Integration: Building an Adaptive Expert Forest for Pre-Trained Model-based Class-Incremental Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) and Eq. (<a href="#S3.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ Hierarchical Construction. ‣ 3.2 Our Method: Semantic-guided Adaptive Expert Forest ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ From Isolation to Integration: Building an Adaptive Expert Forest for Pre-Trained Model-based Class-Incremental Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>), until a single tree root is formed. Finally, the main procedure merges the roots of all trees into a single global expert (Lines 16-17) as per Eq. (<a href="#S3.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ Hierarchical Construction. ‣ 3.2 Our Method: Semantic-guided Adaptive Expert Forest ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ From Isolation to Integration: Building an Adaptive Expert Forest for Pre-Trained Model-based Class-Incremental Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>), completing the full hierarchy.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Online Adaptive Inference.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ Appendix A Detailed Implementation of SAEF Operations ‣ From Isolation to Integration: Building an Adaptive Expert Forest for Pre-Trained Model-based Class-Incremental Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> details the online prediction mechanism for a given input sample <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. The <span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Inference</span> procedure first identifies the set of all relevant experts, <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> (Lines 4-8). This involves starting with the global expert and then traversing each of the <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> trees to find the optimal path using the recursive helper function <span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FindPath</span> (Line 6). The <span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FindPath</span> procedure (Lines 23-35) implements the entropy-guided traversal, including the early-exit mechanism controlled by the threshold <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> (Line 26). Once the set of activated experts is finalized, the algorithm computes the predictive distribution <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and corresponding confidence (entropy <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>) for each expert (Lines 10-14). Finally, it calculates the normalized, confidence-based fusion weights <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and computes the final prediction as a weighted average of all activated experts’ outputs (Lines 16-20), directly implementing the fusion logic from Eq. (<a href="#S3.E14" title="Equation 14 ‣ Adaptive Inference. ‣ 3.2 Our Method: Semantic-guided Adaptive Expert Forest ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ From Isolation to Integration: Building an Adaptive Expert Forest for Pre-Trained Model-based Class-Incremental Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) and Eq. (<a href="#S3.E15" title="Equation 15 ‣ Adaptive Inference. ‣ 3.2 Our Method: Semantic-guided Adaptive Expert Forest ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ From Isolation to Integration: Building an Adaptive Expert Forest for Pre-Trained Model-based Class-Incremental Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Theoretical Speedup Calculation</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our experiments, we report the theoretical speedup of SAEF’s inference process relative to a baseline ’Flat Ensemble’ method. The baseline requires querying all <math id="A3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> available task-specific adapters. Therefore, its computational complexity is proportional to <math id="A3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The inference process of SAEF involves two main steps:</p>
<ol id="A3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="A3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A mandatory forward pass through the global root adapter, <math id="A3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A parallel search within each of the <math id="A3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> conceptual trees. The average number of adapters queried per tree is determined by the average search depth, denoted as <math id="A3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="A3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, the total number of adapter queries for SAEF on average is <math id="A3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+K\times\bar{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+K\times\bar{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The theoretical speedup (<math id="A3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>) is defined as the ratio of the number of queries required by the baseline to the number of queries required by SAEF. It is formulated as:</p>
<table id="A3.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\frac{\text{Queries}_{\text{Baseline}}}{\text{Queries}_{\text{SAEF}}}=\frac{N}{1+K\cdot\bar{d}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mtext>Queries</mtext><mtext>Baseline</mtext></msub><msub><mtext>Queries</mtext><mtext>SAEF</mtext></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>N</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\frac{\text{Queries}_{\text{Baseline}}}{\text{Queries}_{\text{SAEF}}}=\frac{N}{1+K\cdot\bar{d}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of task-specific adapters (leaf nodes), <math id="A3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of conceptual clusters (trees), and <math id="A3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is the average search depth, which is controlled by the entropy threshold <math id="A3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{e}</annotation></semantics></math>. This formula provides a principled way to estimate the efficiency gains from SAEF’s hierarchical structure and its adaptive search mechanism.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Performance Stability Across Multiple Runs</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the main paper, our experiments adhere to the standard protocol from Rebuffi et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, employing a fixed random seed of 1993 to determine the class order for the incremental tasks. To rigorously assess the robustness of our method against variations in task composition and ordering, this section extends our analysis by repeating the experiments on ImageNet-R with five different random seeds: {1993, 1994, 1995, 1996, 1997}.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This procedure generates five distinct result sets for each evaluated method. The mean performance for each method is visualized in Figure <a href="#A2.F2" title="Figure A2 ‣ Appendix B SAEF Algorithm Pseudocode ‣ From Isolation to Integration: Building an Adaptive Expert Forest for Pre-Trained Model-based Class-Incremental Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A2</span></a>. As the figure illustrates, SAEF consistently outperforms all competing methods across the different random seeds. This confirms the stability and general effectiveness of our structured, semantic hierarchy, irrespective of the specific sequence in which classes are introduced.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Detailed Incremental Performance Analysis</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To provide a more detailed view of SAEF’s performance progression, we present the incremental average accuracy trends against leading CIL methods in Figure <a href="#A2.F1" title="Figure A1 ‣ Appendix B SAEF Algorithm Pseudocode ‣ From Isolation to Integration: Building an Adaptive Expert Forest for Pre-Trained Model-based Class-Incremental Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A1</span></a>.
These plots, based on a ViT-B/16 backbone pre-trained on ImageNet-1K, track the performance as the number of learned classes increases across our four primary benchmarks.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Across all evaluated settings, the plots clearly show that SAEF not only achieves the highest final accuracy but also maintains a consistent and often widening performance margin throughout the entire learning sequence.
For instance, on the challenging ImageNet-R benchmark (Figure <a href="#A2.F1.sf2" title="Figure 1(b) ‣ Figure A1 ‣ Appendix B SAEF Algorithm Pseudocode ‣ From Isolation to Integration: Building an Adaptive Expert Forest for Pre-Trained Model-based Class-Incremental Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(b)</span></a>), the performance of many competing methods degrades more sharply over time, whereas SAEF’s trajectory remains stable and superior.
This robust performance underscores the benefit of SAEF’s hierarchical knowledge organization, which allows for more effective and targeted knowledge reuse compared to methods that treat adapters as an unstructured collection.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Analysis of Resource Consumption</h2>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The construction of the SAEF expert forest is a highly efficient offline process. For a typical experimental setup with 10 tasks, this entire process completes in under a few seconds on a standard GPU, making the computational overhead negligible.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The primary overhead of our method is in storage. To construct the hierarchy, SAEF creates a set of new internal-node adapters. This process nearly doubles the total number of adapters that need to be stored. For example, in a typical 10-task experiment, our method creates an additional 8 adapters to build the hierarchical structure on top of the 10 original ones. However, this represents a deliberate design choice. Given the inherently small storage footprint of each lightweight adapter, this modest increase in storage is a highly favorable trade-off for the significant and repeated gains in inference speed achieved by our adaptive search mechanism.</p>
</div>
</section>
</article>
</div>
</div>
